+
+---
+
+## Smooth Animation & Motion Principles
+
+Every animation in this build — 3D, DOM, and scroll-driven — must feel fluid and physically grounded, never linear or snappy/jerky. Apply these rules globally:
+
+- **Easing over linear**: never use default linear interpolation for anything user-facing. Use eased curves everywhere — `framer-motion`'s built-in easings (`easeInOut`, custom cubic-bezier arrays like `[0.22, 1, 0.36, 1]` for a smooth "ease-out-expo" feel) for DOM/UI motion, and manual easing functions (`easeOutCubic`, `easeInOutSine`, or damped-spring math) for anything animated inside `useFrame`.
+- **Damped springs over instant snaps**: any value that changes in response to user input (camera position, parallax offset, hover states, mouse-repel particles) should ease toward its target using a spring or exponential-decay lerp (`current += (target - current) * damping`, with a frame-rate-independent damping factor using `delta` from `useFrame`), not jump or animate at a fixed linear rate.
+- **Frame-rate independence**: all `useFrame`-driven motion must scale by `delta` (the time since the last frame), not assume a fixed 60fps — this keeps animation speed consistent across different hardware/refresh rates, which matters since this may run on lower-powered devices per the mobile-degradation requirement above.
+- **Staggered, cascading entrances**: any group of elements appearing together (hero text lines, skills cards, nav links) should animate in with a staggered delay (`framer-motion`'s `staggerChildren`) rather than all at once, for a more choreographed feel.
+- **Scroll-linked animation smoothing**: scroll-driven camera/UI changes (the scroll-linked camera movement, active-nav-link highlighting) should be smoothed/interpolated rather than snapping directly to scroll position — lerp the displayed value toward the scroll-derived target each frame so fast scrolling doesn't cause jarring jumps.
+- **Hover/tap micro-interactions**: buttons, cards, and icon-links should have subtle, quick eased transitions on hover/tap (scale, glow-intensity, border-gradient shift) — around 150–300ms with an ease-out curve — so interactions feel responsive but not abrupt.
+- **No competing/conflicting animations**: ensure state-machine-driven animations (like the camera dolly-zoom `idle → zooming-in → settled`) fully own their target values while active, so they don't fight with the idle parallax/drift animations running in the same frame loop — gate or blend between them explicitly rather than letting both write to the same transform simultaneously.
+
+---
+
+## Code Requirements
+
+- Return full React code starting with standard imports (`import React, { useRef, useState, useMemo, useEffect } from 'react'`, plus all R3F/drei/postprocessing/framer-motion/lucide-react imports).
+- All custom shaders must be complete, valid GLSL strings embedded via drei's `shaderMaterial`, registered with `extend()`.
+- All Three.js geometries/materials must use real, valid `@react-three/fiber` JSX primitives (`<sphereGeometry>`, `<meshStandardMaterial>`, `<instancedMesh>`, etc.) — no invented components.
